{"availability":"available","full":"MaxHalford/sorobn","name":"sorobn","description":"🧮 Bayesian networks in Python","category":"Developer tools","language":"Python","stars":270,"forks":39,"created":"2020-05-05T13:00:36Z","updated":"2026-09-11T09:00:50Z","license":"MIT","branch":"master","homepage":"https://sorobn.streamlit.app","topics":["bayesian-network"],"demo":"https://sorobn.streamlit.app/","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T15:33:47.850Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-09T15:33:47.850Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-09T15:33:47.850Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-07T13:35:08.349Z","stars":270,"forks":39},{"at":"2026-10-09T15:33:47.850Z","stars":270,"forks":39}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["This is an unambitious Python library for working with Bayesian networks. For serious usage, you should probably be using a more established project, such as pomegranate, pgmpy, bnlearn (which is built on the latter), or even PyMC. There's also the well-documented bnlearn package in R. Hey, you could even go medieval and use something like Netica — I'm just jesting, they actually have a nice tutorial on Bayesian networks. By the way, if you're not familiar with Bayesian networks, then I highly recommend Patrick Winston's MIT courses on probabilistic inference (part 1, part 2).","The main goal of this project is to be used for educational purposes. As such, more emphasis is put on tidyness and conciseness than on performance. I find libraries such as pomegranate are wonderful. But, they literally contain several thousand lines of non-obvious code, at the detriment of simplicity and ease of comprehension. I've also put some effort into designing a slick API that makes full use of pandas. Although performance is not the main focus of this library, it is reasonably efficient and should be able to satisfy most use cases in a timely manner."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/MaxHalford/sorobn/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"f99769d7a8fe9be2668df3f768aeadd105506b97fcb881bafdb2538c4cbedfd2","extractedAt":"2026-10-07T13:35:08.498Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-07T13:35:08.498Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"This is an unambitious Python library for working with Bayesian networks. For serious usage, you should probably be using a more established project, such as pomegranate, pgmpy, bnlearn (which is built on the latter), or even PyMC. There's also the well-documented bnlearn package in R. Hey, you could even go medieval and use something like Netica — I'm just jesting, they actually have a nice tutorial on Bayesian networks. By the way, if you're not familiar with Bayesian networks, then I highly recommend Patrick Winston's MIT courses on probabilistic inference (part 1, part 2).","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/MaxHalford/sorobn/blob/master/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[],"fingerprint":"f99769d7a8fe9be2668df3f768aeadd105506b97fcb881bafdb2538c4cbedfd2","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/MaxHalford/sorobn/blob/master/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-07T13:35:08.499Z"},"overviewRepoUpdated":"2026-09-11T09:00:50Z","readmeSnapshot":{"version":1,"status":"saved","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/MaxHalford/sorobn/blob/master/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-07T13:35:08.500Z","hash":"f99769d7a8fe9be2668df3f768aeadd105506b97fcb881bafdb2538c4cbedfd2","originalBytes":31577,"storedBytes":31577,"truncated":false,"searchTerms":["false","true","calls","alarm","burglary","python","amount","john","mary","earthquake","values","status","fit","missing","bn.probability","given","probability","star","variables","title","value","sorobn","variable","bins","city","dtype","event","network","observations","pd.dataframe","distribution","each","edges","method","movie","networks","sample","structure","column","example","pandas","pprint","sorobn.bayesnet","year","bayesian","data","disaggregation","explicit","float64","interval","name","other","proba","samples"